[s0l0!-header][h1]Two-Dimensional Arrays[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Multi-dimensional arrays may be initialized by specifying bracketed values for each row. 
Following is an array with 2 rows and 3 columns: [code format="cpp" ]int x[2][3] = {
  {2, 3, 4}, // 1st row
  {8, 9, 10} // 2nd row
};[/code]
You can also write the same initialization using just one row.[code format="cpp" ]int x[2][3] = {{2, 3, 4}, {8, 9, 10}};[/code]
The elements are accessed by using the row index and column index of the array. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="221"]int x[2][3] = {{2, 3, 4}, {8, 9, 10}};
cout << [b]x[0][2][/b] << endl;
[/code]
[note]The first index 0 refers to the first row. The second index 2 refers to the 3rd element of the first row, which is 4. [/note]
